is the case where we found 5, and this inner one

01:58:06.320 --> 01:58:10.240
is the case where we found bigger than 5.

01:58:10.240 --> 01:58:12.420
And this is the case we found bigger than 3,

01:58:12.420 --> 01:58:14.699
but not bigger than 5.

01:58:14.699 --> 01:58:18.760
So that's what this is showing you is the client.

01:58:18.760 --> 01:58:20.239
So here we call find.

01:58:20.239 --> 01:58:23.500
So we're the client code and we're handling null.

01:58:23.500 --> 01:58:27.619
That's what this is showing.

01:58:27.619 --> 01:58:31.539
If, on the other hand, we have an option, we can do this.

01:58:31.539 --> 01:58:33.159
So we call find.

01:58:33.159 --> 01:58:36.980
And then, because we get an option back, we can call or else.

01:58:36.979 --> 01:58:44.559
So this is actually a function that's defined on the option type.

01:58:44.559 --> 01:58:49.979
Or I should probably properly because of properly call that method.

01:58:49.979 --> 01:58:52.979
So this is the option type that we get back from find and

01:58:52.979 --> 01:58:57.239
then we call or else method on the option type.

01:58:57.239 --> 01:59:01.519
And or else is gonna be implemented to actually return null,

01:59:01.519 --> 01:59:05.519
I'm sorry, return none if the result is done.

01:59:05.520 --> 01:59:07.180
So none will just return none.

01:59:07.180 --> 01:59:09.920
And I'm sorry, I said that wrong.

01:59:09.920 --> 01:59:14.900
If I got a sum back then I would just return the thing I got back.

01:59:14.900 --> 01:59:15.900
Right?

01:59:15.900 --> 01:59:20.240
And if I got a none back then I'm going to replace it with this expression which also

01:59:20.240 --> 01:59:25.000
has to have type option .

01:59:25.000 --> 01:59:31.120
So this will call the function once and then if it's returning none I call the function

01:59:31.120 --> 01:59:34.740
again with my different .

01:59:34.739 --> 01:59:37.979
and this time I can reduce what I'm printing.

01:59:37.979 --> 01:59:42.119
My print cases are just both collapsed into this one here.

01:59:43.619 --> 01:59:44.539
So much better.

01:59:48.500 --> 01:59:51.619
So the compiler doesn't support null checking

01:59:51.619 --> 01:59:53.699
and you can't call methods on null.

01:59:55.420 --> 01:59:56.920
You get a null pointer exception.

01:59:56.920 --> 01:59:58.399
We're used to that from Java.

02:00:01.739 --> 02:00:04.699
