It complains in arithmetic exception by 0.

02:00:57.280 --> 02:00:58.880
So here what I can do is check.

02:00:59.039 --> 02:01:02.240
If I got a zero for the denominator, I can return none.

02:01:02.239 --> 02:01:08.539
right? And if I got something other than zero from the denominator, I can

02:01:08.539 --> 02:01:12.939
do the division and wrap it in a sum.

02:01:17.119 --> 02:01:24.359
So there's also this function option.when and option is also option.unless.

02:01:24.359 --> 02:01:30.679
So this when and unless is a way to pass a predicate.

02:01:30.679 --> 02:01:36.920
So it will only do this part if this succeeds, so m not equals 0.

02:01:36.920 --> 02:01:39.380
Otherwise it will return not, not n, right?

02:01:39.380 --> 02:01:40.739
And unless is the opposite.

02:01:40.739 --> 02:01:45.899
So unless m equals 0, return n divided by n,

02:01:45.899 --> 02:01:49.199
I'm sorry, n divided by m, and then otherwise return n.

02:01:54.359 --> 02:02:05.039
So if I have, if I wanted to divide an optional int safely, so I start off with this four,

02:02:05.039 --> 02:02:10.579
and I want to divide by two, I can call map on my option.

02:02:10.579 --> 02:02:12.339
So this is an option of an int.

02:02:12.339 --> 02:02:14.380
It holds a four, in this case.

02:02:14.380 --> 02:02:20.420
If I call map, remember that if I do this, this is just using the parameter, right?

02:02:20.420 --> 02:02:21.979
So this is just shorthand.

02:02:21.979 --> 02:02:23.839
and it's calling safeDivide.

02:02:24.119 --> 02:02:27.239
So whatever is wrapped by this option, if anything,

02:02:27.659 --> 02:02:29.979
is passed as the first parameter to safeDivide.

02:02:30.199 --> 02:02:31.939
And that's going to return back an option,

02:02:32.099 --> 02:02:35.599
so I'm going to get an option of an option of an int.

02:02:36.939 --> 02:02:42.319
If I do that, if I call i.map and safeDivide with a 0,

02:02:42.679 --> 02:02:46.179
I should get also an option of an option of an int.

02:02:46.179 --> 02:02:50.079
In this case, because 4 can be divided by 2,

02:02:50.079 --> 02:02:53.840
I'm going to get two.

02:02:53.840 --> 02:02:55.340
It's going to be my answer.

02:02:55.340 --> 02:02:57.100
And with this double wrapping.

02:02:57.100 --> 02:02:59.899
And then over here, because I can't divide by zero,

02:02:59.899 --> 02:03:05.260
I'm going to get none, right?

02:03:05.260 --> 02:03:07.239
But we don't like nested options.

02:03:07.239 --> 02:03:10.059
And if you remember, we had a function on lists,

02:03:10.059 --> 02:03:13.720
a method on lists that we could use.

02:03:13.720 --> 02:03:16.019
So we can say flat map.

02:03:16.019 --> 02:03:17.899
And flat map will flatten this thing.

02:03:17.899 --> 02:03:22.559
So if I have an option of an option of an int,

02:03:22.559 --> 02:03:24.239
so if I took this and called flatten,

02:03:24.239 --> 02:03:29.399
it would get back a single option wrapping in it.

02:03:29.399 --> 02:03:31.339
Here, I can call flatten app.

02:03:31.339 --> 02:03:33.920
And the return type of this thing has to be an option.

02:03:33.920 --> 02:03:36.559
And this will just give me one flattened option.

02:03:42.739 --> 02:03:47.659
We talked about nil at the end of the first half.

