This is just relating it to that language.

02:09:23.500 --> 02:09:24.980
So here's our summary.

02:09:24.979 --> 02:09:29.939
option type represents presence or absence of data

02:09:29.939 --> 02:09:34.519
either type represents alternative results so we didn't talk about either

02:09:34.519 --> 02:09:36.819
but either is defined usually as

02:09:36.819 --> 02:09:42.419
I think we might see ethers later

02:09:42.419 --> 02:09:46.459
in the course but like with option we have either something

02:09:46.459 --> 02:09:49.719
or nothing or some or none I guess for either

02:09:49.720 --> 02:09:55.280
we have either an A or a B

02:09:55.280 --> 02:09:59.440
and this means that this is a sum type

02:09:59.440 --> 02:10:01.159
actually so if I have sum

02:10:01.159 --> 02:10:03.579
val x

02:10:03.579 --> 02:10:07.199
either AB equals

02:10:07.199 --> 02:10:08.820
and then sum code here

02:10:08.820 --> 02:10:10.460
then I'm either going to have

02:10:10.460 --> 02:10:12.320
this will actually be a left

02:10:12.320 --> 02:10:14.960
A

02:10:14.960 --> 02:10:19.859
left A

02:10:19.859 --> 02:10:22.180
or right

02:10:22.180 --> 02:10:25.680
B.

02:10:28.420 --> 02:10:29.980
I'm using sort of

02:10:29.980 --> 02:10:31.840
pseudo code here when I write this, but

02:10:31.840 --> 02:10:33.220
it's either going to be a left

02:10:33.220 --> 02:10:35.060
type or a right type.

02:10:35.819 --> 02:10:37.100
Oftentimes in

02:10:37.100 --> 02:10:39.560
production Scala code, you might see

02:10:39.560 --> 02:10:42.220
either

02:10:42.220 --> 02:10:47.560
E A.

02:10:48.100 --> 02:10:51.760
The reason we do that is because this E denotes error.

02:10:52.500 --> 02:10:54.500
So the convention, and it's not required,

02:10:54.640 --> 02:10:56.320
but we usually put the error on the left.

02:10:57.020 --> 02:10:59.900
And so we often say that either is right biased

02:10:59.900 --> 02:11:00.900
because of the right one.

02:11:00.900 --> 02:11:04.100
So a valid value would be a right A,

02:11:05.159 --> 02:11:10.140
which is kind of corresponding to a sum A.

02:11:10.140 --> 02:11:15.119
But what LEFT E would give you here is the ability to capture an error

02:11:15.119 --> 02:11:17.020
but not have it throw an exception.

02:11:17.440 --> 02:11:19.740
You can still retain what the error was.

02:11:20.119 --> 02:11:23.320
So this is why we often use this in Corruption in Scala Code.

02:11:24.619 --> 02:11:28.880
Then there's all kinds of variations on this theme in the cat's effect library.

02:11:29.380 --> 02:11:31.160
But I don't remember what they all are.

02:11:31.220 --> 02:11:33.400
There's all these ways you can chain the errors together

02:11:33.400 --> 02:11:35.680
so if you have multiple errors, you can list them out

02:11:35.680 --> 02:11:38.800
and report them to logs and whatever.

02:11:40.140 --> 02:11:46.840
So our option and our either are going to support higher order functions. I might

02:11:46.840 --> 02:11:52.140
have said a minute ago so either is going to be right bias. So map will map

02:11:52.140 --> 02:11:58.700
a right to whatever it'll pass a function, it will operate on

02:11:58.700 --> 02:12:06.100
an A. And if you have a left and you call map it'll just return whatever the

02:12:06.100 --> 02:12:11.140
left is holding. It doesn't do any transformation. You can also call left map, which will actually

02:12:11.140 --> 02:12:16.400
map the error or the thing on the left to some other value. So it's right biased.

02:12:19.700 --> 02:12:27.079
This gives us the ability to do things like or else. So if I have an option and I call or else,

02:12:27.280 --> 02:12:32.320
I'm going to get whatever. We saw this earlier, but if I have a none and I call or else,

02:12:32.319 --> 02:12:34.119
that I'm going to get whatever I passed.

02:12:34.420 --> 02:12:36.119
And actually, it's going to use non-strictness,

02:12:36.199 --> 02:12:37.259
so it's not going to evaluate.

02:12:37.899 --> 02:12:40.340
If we look at or else,

02:12:40.439 --> 02:12:47.059
and I don't remember what it's actually called,

02:12:47.139 --> 02:12:48.159
but if I say x,

02:12:49.239 --> 02:12:51.859
I'm going to use this syntax,

02:12:52.599 --> 02:12:56.920
option x.

02:13:00.199 --> 02:13:01.739
And that's going to return

02:13:01.739 --> 02:13:03.500
option

02:13:03.500 --> 02:13:06.279
x.

02:13:06.760 --> 02:13:08.840
So what's this doing? This syntax

02:13:08.840 --> 02:13:10.899
here means that

02:13:10.899 --> 02:13:12.840
x produces an option of x, but

02:13:12.840 --> 02:13:14.859
it may not actually execute this because

02:13:14.859 --> 02:13:15.920
this is non-strict.

02:13:17.500 --> 02:13:18.920
So if I have

02:13:18.920 --> 02:13:21.099
a sum

02:13:21.099 --> 02:13:23.159
and I pass this expression,

02:13:23.319 --> 02:13:25.099
this will be a thunk that never gets evaluated

02:13:25.099 --> 02:13:27.099
and then it's a little bit more efficient.

02:13:27.460 --> 02:13:28.659
So this is how

02:13:28.659 --> 02:13:31.319
non-strictness shows up

02:13:31.319 --> 02:13:35.939
in the wild. So, if you ever see that, like the syntax in Scala, that's what's

02:13:35.939 --> 02:13:41.899
going on, so it won't actually evaluate. This is actually very useful.

